1qwk|n|A species of fictitious writing, originally composed in meter in the Romance dialects, and afterward in prose, such as the tales of the court of Arthur, and of Amadis of Gaul; hence, any fictitious and wonderful tale; a sort of novel, especially one which treats of surprising adventures usually befalling a hero or a heroine; a tale of extravagant adventures, of love, and the like. ; An adventure, or series of extraordinary events, resembling those narrated in romances; as, his courtship, or his life, was a romance. ; A dreamy, imaginative habit of mind; a disposition to ignore what is real; as, a girl full of romance. ; The languages, or rather the several dialects, which were originally forms of popular or vulgar Latin, and have now developed into Italian. Spanish, French, etc. (called the Romanic languages). ; A short lyric tale set to music; a song or short instrumental piece in ballad style; a romanza.	a:b30,98,8~b30,98,8	n:1siy,44,3;8k,6,4~8k,6,4;1siy,44,3	N:hus,109,5;dyw,97,1;dbk,94,6;372,58,3;1tqi,56,4;1y5i,50,2;1554,45,4~dbk,94,6;hus,109,5;1tqi,56,4;1554,45,4;372,58,3;1y5i,50,2;dyw,97,1
1qwk|a|Of or pertaining to the language or dialects known as Romance.	j:m5s,90,1~m5s,90,1
